    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                David is a boda boda rider in Nakuru.
                On his first day of work he earns Ksh <m>600</m>.
                As he builds his route and gains regular customers,
                his daily earnings increase by Ksh <m>80</m> each day.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>How much does David earn on day <m>12</m>?</p></li>
                    <li><p>What are his total earnings over the first four weeks (28 days)?</p></li>
                    <li><p>On which day does he first earn more than Ksh <m>2000</m> in a single day?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                This is an arithmetic sequence with first term <m>a = 600</m> and common difference <m>d = 80</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                <mrow>T_{12} \amp =\; a + 11d = 600 + 11 \times 80 = 600 + 880 = \text{Ksh } 1480</mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                <mrow>S_{28} \amp =\; \frac{28}{2}(2a + 27d)</mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp =\; 14(1200 + 2160)</mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp =\; 14 \times 3360 = \text{Ksh } 47{,}040</mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            We need <m>T_n = 600 + (n-1) \times 80 &gt; 2000</m>.
                            <md>
                                <mrow>(n-1) \times 80 \amp &gt;\; 1400</mrow>
                                <mrow>n-1 \amp &gt;\; 17.5</mrow>
                                <mrow>n \amp \geq\; 19</mrow>
                            </md>
                            David first earns more than Ksh 2000 on <em>day 19</em>.
                            Check: <m>T_{19} = 600 + 18 \times 80 = 600 + 1440 = 2040</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A plant disease is detected in a maize farm near Eldoret.
                On Day 1, <m>4</m> plants are infected.
                The number of infected plants triples every day.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>How many plants are infected on Day 6?</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find the total number of plants infected over the first 6 days.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The farm has 3000 maize plants. On which day will all plants potentially be infected?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                This is a geometric sequence with first term <m>a = 4</m> and common ratio <m>r = 3</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                <mrow>T_6 \amp =\; 4 \times 3^5 = 4 \times 243 = 972 \text{ plants}</mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                <mrow>S_6 \amp =\; \frac{4(3^6 - 1)}{3 - 1} = \frac{4 \times 728}{2} = 1456 \text{ plants}</mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            We need <m>4 \times 3^{n-1} \geq 3000</m>, so <m>3^{n-1} \geq 750</m>.
                            <md>
                                <mrow>3^6 \amp =\; 729 &lt; 750</mrow>
                                <mrow>3^7 \amp =\; 2187 &gt; 750</mrow>
                            </md>
                            So <m>n - 1 = 7</m>, giving <m>n = 8</m>.
                            By <em>Day 8</em>, the infection can potentially reach all 3000 plants.
                            This shows why early intervention is critical — the disease spreads explosively.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Amina needs to repay a loan and is offered two plans.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <em>Plan A:</em> Pay Ksh <m>1500</m> in month 1,
                            with payments increasing by Ksh <m>300</m> each month (arithmetic).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <em>Plan B:</em> Pay Ksh <m>1000</m> in month 1,
                            with payments increasing by <m>40\%</m> each month (geometric).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Write down the monthly payments for each plan over 6 months.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Calculate the total repaid under each plan over 6 months.</p></li>
                    <li><p>In which month do Plan B's monthly payments first exceed Plan A's?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <em>Plan A</em> (arithmetic, <m>a = 1500</m>, <m>d = 300</m>):
                        </p>
                        <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                            <row header="yes">
                                <cell>Month</cell>
                                <cell>1</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>3</cell>
                                <cell>4</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>6</cell>
                            </row>
                            <row>
                                <cell>Payment (Ksh)</cell>
                                <cell>1500</cell><cell>1800</cell><cell>2100</cell>
                                <cell>2400</cell><cell>2700</cell><cell>3000</cell>
                            </row>
                        </tabular>
                        <p>
                            <em>Plan B</em> (geometric, <m>a = 1000</m>, <m>r = 1.4</m>):
                        </p>
                        <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                            <row header="yes">
                                <cell>Month</cell>
                                <cell>1</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>3</cell>
                                <cell>4</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>6</cell>
                            </row>
                            <row>
                                <cell>Payment (Ksh)</cell>
                                <cell>1000</cell><cell>1400</cell><cell>1960</cell>
                                <cell>2744</cell><cell>3842</cell><cell>5378</cell>
                            </row>
                        </tabular>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <em>Plan A total:</em>
                            <md>
                                <mrow>S_6 \amp =\; \frac{6}{2}(1500 + 3000) = 3 \times 4500 = \text{Ksh } 13{,}500</mrow>
                            </md>
                            <em>Plan B total</em> (using <m>1.4^6 \approx 7.530</m>):
                            <md>
                                <mrow>S_6 \amp =\; \frac{1000(1.4^6 - 1)}{1.4 - 1} = \frac{1000 \times 6.530}{0.4} \approx \text{Ksh } 16{,}325</mrow>
                            </md>
                            <em>Plan A is cheaper overall by approximately Ksh 2825.</em>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Comparing monthly payments:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\text{Month 3: } &amp; \text{A} = 2100, \quad \text{B} = 1960 \quad (\text{A still higher})</mrow>
                                <mrow>\text{Month 4: } &amp; \text{A} = 2400, \quad \text{B} = 2744 \quad (\text{B overtakes A})</mrow>
                            </md>
                            Plan B's monthly payments first exceed Plan A's in <em>Month 4</em>.
                            Although Plan B starts cheaper, it accelerates quickly and becomes more expensive.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The population of a town was <m>120{,}000</m> in 2020.
                    It grows at a rate of <m>2.5\%</m> per year.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Write a geometric sequence for the town's population, starting from 2020.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find the population in 2025, to the nearest hundred.</p></li>
                        <li><p>In which year does the population first exceed <m>150{,}000</m>?</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>120{,}000 \times (1.025)^{n-1}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Approximately <m>135{,}800</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>2030</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>a = 120{,}000</m>, <m>r = 1.025</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P_n = 120{,}000 \times (1.025)^{n-1}</m>, where <m>n = 1</m> is the year 2020.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                2025 is year <m>n = 6</m>:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>P_6 \amp =\; 120{,}000 \times (1.025)^5 \approx 120{,}000 \times 1.1314 \approx 135{,}768</mrow>
                                </md>
                                To the nearest hundred: <m>135{,}800</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We need <m>120{,}000 \times (1.025)^{n-1} &gt; 150{,}000</m>,
                                so <m>(1.025)^{n-1} &gt; 1.25</m>.
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>(1.025)^9 \amp \approx\; 1.249 &lt; 1.25</mrow>
                                    <mrow>(1.025)^{10} \amp \approx\; 1.280 &gt; 1.25</mrow>
                                </md>
                                So <m>n - 1 = 10</m>, giving <m>n = 11</m>, which is the year <m>2020 + 10 = \mathbf{2030}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Otieno starts a savings plan.
                    In week 1 he saves Ksh <m>200</m>, and each week he saves Ksh <m>50</m> more than the previous week.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>How much does he save in week 10?</p></li>
                        <li><p>What is his total savings after 20 weeks?</p></li>
                        <li><p>He wants to buy a laptop costing Ksh <m>25{,}000</m>. After how many complete weeks will he have saved enough?</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Ksh <m>650</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Ksh <m>13{,}500</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>After <m>29</m> complete weeks</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>a = 200</m>, <m>d = 50</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>T_{10} \amp =\; 200 + 9 \times 50 = 650 \text{ Ksh}</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>S_{20} \amp =\; \frac{20}{2}(2 \times 200 + 19 \times 50) = 10(400 + 950) = \text{Ksh } 13{,}500</mrow>
                                </md>
                                This is less than Ksh 25,000, so he cannot afford the laptop after 20 weeks.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We need <m>S_n = \dfrac{n}{2}(400 + 50(n-1)) = \dfrac{n}{2}(350 + 50n) &gt; 25{,}000</m>.
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>25n^2 + 175n - 25{,}000 \amp &gt;\; 0</mrow>
                                    <mrow>n^2 + 7n - 1000 \amp &gt;\; 0</mrow>
                                </md>
                                <m>S_n = \frac{n}{2}(2 \times 200 + (n-1) \times 50) = \frac{n}{2}(350 + 50n) = n(175 + 25n)</m>.

                                Using the quadratic formula: <m>n = \dfrac{-7 + \sqrt{49 + 4000}}{2} \approx \dfrac{-7 + 63.9}{2} \approx 28.5</m>.

                                Check by substitution:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>S_{28} \amp =\; 28(175 + 700) = 28 \times 875 = 24{,}500 &lt; 25{,}000</mrow>
                                    <mrow>S_{29} \amp =\; 29(175 + 725) = 29 \times 900 = 26{,}100 &gt; 25{,}000</mrow>
                                </md>
                                Otieno needs <em>29 complete weeks</em>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Amina deposits Ksh <m>5{,}000</m> into an M-Pesa savings account
                    that earns <m>1.5\%</m> monthly compound interest. She makes no further deposits.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Write a formula for the amount in the account after <m>n</m> months.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find the amount after 12 months, to the nearest shilling.</p></li>
                        <li><p>After how many complete months will the account first exceed Ksh <m>6{,}000</m>?</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>A_n = 5000 \times (1.015)^n</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Ksh <m>5{,}978</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>After 13 months</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Each month the balance is multiplied by <m>1.015</m>:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>A_n \amp =\; 5000 \times (1.015)^n</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>A_{12} \amp =\; 5000 \times (1.015)^{12} \approx 5000 \times 1.19562 \approx \text{Ksh } 5{,}978</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We need <m>5000 \times (1.015)^n &gt; 6000</m>, so <m>(1.015)^n &gt; 1.2</m>.
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>(1.015)^{12} \amp \approx\; 1.1956 &lt; 1.2</mrow>
                                    <mrow>(1.015)^{13} \amp \approx\; 1.2136 &gt; 1.2</mrow>
                                </md>
                                The account first exceeds Ksh 6000 after <em>13 months</em>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A patient is prescribed a <m>200</m> mg tablet to be taken once daily.
                    Medical studies show that by the time the next tablet is taken,
                    exactly half of the drug has been processed by the body.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Write down the total amount of drug in the patient's body
                                immediately after taking tablets 1, 2, 3, and 4.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Show that the total drug in the body immediately after the <m>n</m>th tablet is
                                <me>D_n = 400\!\left(1 - \left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right)^n\right) \text{ mg}</me>
                                and use this to find <m>D_5</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Find the maximum total amount of drug <m>D_\infty</m> that can ever
                                accumulate in the body under this daily dosing regime.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The safe maximum concentration is <m>380</m> mg.
                                Is this dosing regimen safe? What advice would you give the prescribing doctor?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>200, \ 300, \ 350, \ 375</m> mg</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>D_5 = 387.5</m> mg</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>D_\infty = 400</m> mg</p></li>
                        <li><p>Not safe — the long-term accumulation of 400 mg exceeds the 380 mg limit.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                After each tablet, half of what was present remains, then a new 200 mg dose is added:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>D_1 \amp =\; 200 \text{ mg}</mrow>
                                    <mrow>D_2 \amp =\; \tfrac{1}{2}(200) + 200 = 100 + 200 = 300 \text{ mg}</mrow>
                                    <mrow>D_3 \amp =\; \tfrac{1}{2}(300) + 200 = 150 + 200 = 350 \text{ mg}</mrow>
                                    <mrow>D_4 \amp =\; \tfrac{1}{2}(350) + 200 = 175 + 200 = 375 \text{ mg}</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The total after the <m>n</m>th tablet is the sum of all doses, each decayed by the appropriate power of <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>D_n \amp =\; 200\!\left(1 + \tfrac{1}{2} + \tfrac{1}{4} + \cdots + \left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right)^{n-1}\right)</mrow>
                                    <mrow> \amp =\; 200 \times \frac{1 - \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^n}{1 - \frac{1}{2}} = 400\!\left(1 - \left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right)^n\right)</mrow>
                                </md>
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>D_5 \amp =\; 400\!\left(1 - \tfrac{1}{32}\right) = 400 \times \tfrac{31}{32} = 387.5 \text{ mg}</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                As <m>n \to \infty</m>, <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^n \to 0</m>, so:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>D_\infty \amp =\; 400 \text{ mg}</mrow>
                                </md>
                                Alternatively, using <m>S_\infty = \dfrac{a}{1-r} = \dfrac{200}{\frac{1}{2}} = 400</m> mg.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Since <m>D_\infty = 400 \text{ mg} &gt; 380 \text{ mg}</m>, the regimen is <em>not safe</em>.
                                The doctor should reduce the daily dose (for example to 190 mg, giving <m>D_\infty = 380</m> mg)
                                or increase the time between doses to allow more of the drug to clear.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Mama Wanjiku's vegetable stall in Machakos makes a profit of Ksh <m>1{,}800</m> in its first week.
                    Each week, her profit increases by Ksh <m>250</m> as she builds her customer base.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>In which week does her weekly profit first exceed Ksh <m>5{,}000</m>?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find her total profit over the first year (52 weeks).</p></li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                She needs to save Ksh <m>200{,}000</m> to expand her stall.
                                After how many complete weeks of trading will she have accumulated enough profit?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Week 14</p></li>
                        <li><p>Ksh <m>425{,}100</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>After <m>34</m> complete weeks</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>a = 1800</m>, <m>d = 250</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We need <m>1800 + 250(n-1) &gt; 5000</m>:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>250(n-1) \amp &gt;\; 3200</mrow>
                                    <mrow>n-1 \amp &gt;\; 12.8</mrow>
                                    <mrow>n \amp \geq\; 14</mrow>
                                </md>
                                Her profit first exceeds Ksh 5000 in <em>week 14</em>.
                                Check: <m>T_{14} = 1800 + 13 \times 250 = 5050</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>S_{52} \amp =\; \frac{52}{2}(2 \times 1800 + 51 \times 250)</mrow>
                                    <mrow> \amp =\; 26(3600 + 12{,}750)</mrow>
                                    <mrow> \amp =\; 26 \times 16{,}350 = \text{Ksh } 425{,}100</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>S_n = \dfrac{n}{2}(2 \times 1800 + 250(n-1)) = n(1675 + 125n)</m>.
                                We need <m>n(1675 + 125n) &gt; 200{,}000</m>, i.e. <m>125n^2 + 1675n - 200{,}000 &gt; 0</m>.
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>n \amp =\; \frac{-1675 + \sqrt{1675^2 + 4 \times 125 \times 200{,}000}}{250}</mrow>
                                    <mrow> \amp =\; \frac{-1675 + \sqrt{102{,}805{,}625}}{250} \approx \frac{-1675 + 10{,}139}{250} \approx 33.9</mrow>
                                </md>
                                Check by substitution:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>S_{33} \amp =\; 33(1675 + 4125) = 33 \times 5800 = 191{,}400 &lt; 200{,}000</mrow>
                                    <mrow>S_{34} \amp =\; 34(1675 + 4250) = 34 \times 5925 = 201{,}450 &gt; 200{,}000</mrow>
                                </md>
                                Mama Wanjiku needs <em>34 complete weeks</em> of trading. <m>\checkmark</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

